WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:04.650
Truth is sometimes stranger than fiction,
but when it comes to the stars of the History

00:00:04.650 --> 00:00:08.620
Channel hit Swamp People, truth is also stranger
than reality television.

00:00:08.969 --> 00:00:11.860
Think you know everything there is to know
about these muck dwellers?

00:00:11.870 --> 00:00:15.759
Well, think again, because we're about to
reveal what die-hard fans don't even know

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:16.700
about Swamp People.

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:18.620
Gator bait

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:24.300
In May of 2017, former Swamp People star Roger
A. Rivers Jr. was arrested and charged with

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:29.180
20 counts of...well, pretty much everything,
from illegally selling alligator meat to marijuana

00:00:29.180 --> 00:00:29.680
possession.

00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:33.960
So if you ever thought the cast of Swamp People
must be high to mess around with gators like

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:37.380
that, turns out you were right — at least
when it comes to Roger Rivers

00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:39.060
Quick draw

00:00:39.610 --> 00:00:44.100
Swamp justice apparently moves a lot quicker
than traditional courts, which is something

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:48.100
the operators of a shrimp boat found out the
hard way after Chase Landry shot at them.

00:00:48.380 --> 00:00:52.340
According to the Terrebonne Parish Sheriff's
Office, Landry was in Bayou Chene in September

00:00:52.340 --> 00:00:57.039
of 2016 hunting alligators when he fired at
the passing boaters, allegedly shooting a

00:00:57.040 --> 00:01:01.760
hole into a "plastic gas can which was on
top of the cabin of the commercial shrimp

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:02.260
boat."

00:01:02.500 --> 00:01:06.480
Upon his arrest, Landry claimed "he shot because
the victim refused to slow down and would

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:07.220
have sunk their boat."

00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:09.580
At least he played it safe by shooting a gas
can.

00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:10.980
What's the worst that could have happened?

00:01:12.820 --> 00:01:14.340
"Smile you son of a..."

00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:21.220
Five feet high and rising

00:01:21.880 --> 00:01:25.780
Just one month before his alleged vigilante
attempt to stop a speeding shrimp boat, Chase

00:01:25.790 --> 00:01:30.170
Landry spent five days in and around Baton
Rouge rescuing dozens of people stranded in

00:01:30.170 --> 00:01:33.240
high water during the Louisiana Flood of 2016.

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:37.280
Landry told The Times-Picayune, "Seeing everyone
get together and forgetting about what color

00:01:37.299 --> 00:01:41.670
we are or what this and that one said — that
was thrown to the wayside and what was important

00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:42.170
came out.

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:45.220
… If you see your brother or sister or neighbor
in a bind, you'll help them."

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:47.580
Unless they're speeding in a shrimp boat.

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:50.900
In that case, you know, shoot at the most
explosive thing possible.

00:01:56.880 --> 00:01:57.560
Money shot

00:01:58.220 --> 00:02:02.080
He may sound like a character from The Waterboy,
but Troy Landry is no fool.

00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:06.280
Not only does he operate a successful crawfish
harvesting businesses, he also rakes in the

00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:11.160
cash making roughly 230 public appearances
a year thanks to his fame as a reality TV

00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:11.660
star.

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:13.500
So what has he done with all that money?

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:18.220
Turns out he's built a dream home on the bayou,
which is entirely made of cypress, and boasts

00:02:18.220 --> 00:02:21.360
both an outdoor kitchen and a thousand-square-foot
living room.

00:02:21.610 --> 00:02:26.100
Who would've guessed selling stickers featuring
the phrase "Mudda Fricka" could possibly land

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:27.300
you in the lap of luxury?

00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:29.180
That shirt

00:02:30.020 --> 00:02:34.140
Troy Landry's signature striped polo shirt
has become as much of a staple on Swamp People

00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:36.700
as captioned dialogue and dead alligators.

00:02:36.940 --> 00:02:41.160
But according to Landry, the shirt holds a
secret, hidden in plain sight under everyone's

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:41.660
noses.

00:02:42.100 --> 00:02:45.540
He posted on Facebook, "I know everyone is
wondering why I wear the same shirt?

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:47.180
The answer is: we all do!

00:02:47.420 --> 00:02:49.500
Mine just stands out because of the stripes.

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:50.560
It's for editing purposes.

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:52.440
We bought ten of the same shirt."

00:02:52.820 --> 00:02:56.480
Just think, if the other cast members had
worn more interesting shirts, they might have

00:02:56.480 --> 00:02:57.700
become the star of the show!

00:02:58.640 --> 00:02:59.420
Cruelty claims

00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:03.950
Over the years, Swamp People has come under
fire from animal rights activists.

00:03:03.950 --> 00:03:08.330
The protest site ForceChange.com petitioned
The History Channel to cancel the show, writing,

00:03:08.330 --> 00:03:12.240
"Swamp People does nothing to educate people
about history, it only promotes killing.

00:03:12.420 --> 00:03:16.560
It is very immoral to air a show that depicts
people torturing and killing animals."

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:20.440
The Louisiana Department of Wildlife and Fisheries,
on the other hand, argues that their program

00:03:20.440 --> 00:03:23.280
of "regulated harvest" is actually a conservation
effort.

00:03:23.540 --> 00:03:27.440
According to the department's website, "our
sustained use management program provides

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:32.090
direct economic benefit and incentive to private
landowners, and alligator hunters/farmers

00:03:32.090 --> 00:03:37.340
who lease land, to protect the alligator and
to protect, maintain, and enhance the alligator's

00:03:37.340 --> 00:03:38.460
wetland habitats."

00:03:38.870 --> 00:03:43.720
So far, The History Channel seems to be siding
with Swamp People and not the gators — but

00:03:43.720 --> 00:03:45.600
who knows what changes the future might bring.

00:03:46.420 --> 00:03:47.240
Later, gator!

00:03:48.300 --> 00:03:51.380
Swamp People has actually had a measurable
effect on the alligator meat industry.

00:03:51.580 --> 00:03:55.280
According to a Miami New Times investigation
of the lucrative black market that deals in

00:03:55.290 --> 00:03:59.940
alligator meat and skin, shows like Swamp
People and Gator Boys have "fueled a surge

00:03:59.940 --> 00:04:04.000
in prices as alligator meat became a novelty
in restaurants throughout the South.

00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:10.900
From 2013 to 2015, the price doubled to $8.75
a pound on the wholesale market, and trappers

00:04:10.900 --> 00:04:13.080
couldn't catch enough alligators to meet the
demand."

00:04:13.400 --> 00:04:18.120
The Times also reported that alligator skins
sell for "anywhere from $20 to $40 per foot."

00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:23.300
That's remarkable, not only because a TV show
could have such a real world effect on a commodity

00:04:23.310 --> 00:04:27.450
like that, but also because it means people
are genuinely basing their diet and wardrobe

00:04:27.450 --> 00:04:29.560
choices on something called Swamp People.

00:04:29.840 --> 00:04:30.840
Thanks for watching!

